#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Written takeaways for Genesis 1-27."""

TAKEAWAYS = {
    # ---- Chapter 1 ----
    '1:1': "The Bible opens by assuming God and arguing for nothing. Everything else in it is downstream of this sentence.",
    '1:2': "Before a word is spoken the Spirit is already over the chaos. God does not arrive after the darkness.",
    '1:3': "God speaks and it is. Creation by word sets the pattern for every act of God that follows.",
    '1:4': "\"God saw the light, that it was good.\" The maker inspects his own work and pronounces the verdict himself.",
    '1:5': "Naming is an act of authority, and God names the day and the night before any man names anything.",
    '1:6': "Creation proceeds by separation. Making room is as much a work of God as filling it.",
    '1:7': "\"And it was so.\" Between God's word and the fact there is no distance at all.",
    '1:9': "The sea appears when the water is told where to stop. Limits are what make the world habitable.",
    '1:10': "Dry land and seas are both called good. Nothing in the finished world is merely tolerated.",
    '1:11': "Seed is put inside the thing made. God builds the future into creation rather than remaking it each morning.",
    '1:14': "Sun and moon are given a job: signs, seasons, days and years. Time is a creature with a purpose.",
    '1:16': "\"He made the stars also.\" The lights other nations worshipped are given half a clause and not even named.",
    '1:18': "The lights rule by appointment. Every authority in this book holds office from someone above it.",
    '1:20': "Life is commanded to swarm. Abundance, not bare sufficiency, is the shape of the blessing.",
    '1:21': "The great sea creatures that fill the myths of the neighbours are simply made here, and called good.",
    '1:22': "The first blessing in Scripture falls on fish and birds. Humanity is not the first thing God is generous to.",
    '1:25': "The creeping things are called good before any use for them has been mentioned.",
    '1:26': "\"Let us make man.\" God deliberates before this one, and the deliberation is inside himself.",
    '1:27': "The image is given to male and female alike, and to the whole race before any of it has done anything.",
    '1:28': "Dominion is handed over as a blessing, not seized as a right, and it is exercised under the one who gave it.",
    '1:29': "God feeds his creatures before he asks anything further of them. Provision precedes demand.",
    '1:30': "The beasts are fed from the same hand. God's care does not stop at the edge of what is useful to us.",
    '1:31': "\"Very good\" is said only of the whole. The superlative waits until everything is in place together.",

    # ---- Chapter 2 ----
    '2:1': "\"Finished.\" Creation is a completed work, not an ongoing experiment that might yet be corrected.",
    '2:2': "God rests though nothing has tired him. The rest exists for the sake of the day, not for his sake.",
    '2:3': "The first holy thing in Scripture is a day, not a place, a person or an object.",
    '2:4': "The covenant name LORD appears only now, joined to God. The maker of galaxies is the one who deals by name.",
    '2:5': "The account pauses to say what was missing: rain, and a man to work the ground. Both are about to be supplied.",
    '2:7': "Dust and breath. Man is neither pure matter nor pure spirit, and the dust is named first.",
    '2:8': "The garden was planted before the man was placed in it. He inherits a prepared world.",
    '2:9': "Both trees stand in the same garden. The prohibition is set inside abundance, not instead of it.",
    '2:10': "The river does not pool in Eden but runs out of it in four directions. The garden was never meant to be private.",
    '2:15': "Work is given before the fall, as a charge to tend and guard. Labour is not the curse; its bitterness is.",
    '2:16': "The permission comes first and it is enormous: every tree in the garden.",
    '2:17': "One tree is withheld, with the reason and the penalty stated plainly in advance. Nothing is sprung on him.",
    '2:18': "The first thing God calls \"not good\" is a man on his own, in a world otherwise pronounced good.",
    '2:19': "God brings the animals \"to see what he would call them.\" The delegation is real and its outcome stands.",
    '2:20': "The naming ends in a discovery of lack. Adam learns what he needs by looking at everything that is not it.",
    '2:21': "The man is asleep for the making of his wife. He contributes nothing to her but the loss of a rib.",
    '2:22': "God brings her to him. The first marriage happens because someone else did the giving.",
    '2:23': "The first recorded human words are poetry, and they are about another person rather than himself.",
    '2:24': "Leaving comes before cleaving. A new household outranks the one a man was raised in.",
    '2:25': "Naked and not ashamed. Shame is absent here, which is the measure of what the next chapter costs.",

    # ---- Chapter 3 ----
    '3:1': "The first question recorded in Scripture is a question about what God said.",
    '3:3': "\"Neither shall ye touch it\" was never said. A fence added to the command makes God sound harsher than he is.",
    '3:4': "\"Ye shall not surely die.\" The serpent does not offer a rival religion, only a flat denial of one sentence.",
    '3:5': "The bait is God's own prerogative, offered on terms that leave God out.",
    '3:6': "Good for food, pleasant to the eyes, desirable to make wise. Sin is described as attractive, because it is.",
    '3:7': "Their eyes are opened and the first thing they see is themselves. New knowledge, and it is unbearable.",
    '3:8': "They hide from God among the trees God made. There was never any material for a hiding place but his own gifts.",
    '3:9': "\"Where art thou?\" God asks a question he knows the answer to, and the first move after the fall is his.",
    '3:10': "Adam reports the fear and the nakedness and says nothing about the eating.",
    '3:11': "\"Who told thee that thou wast naked?\" The question goes after where the new knowledge came from.",
    '3:12': "The blame passes through the woman and stops at God: \"the woman whom thou gavest.\"",
    '3:13': "She hands it on to the serpent, and there the chain runs out. Someone has to say \"I did eat\" at last.",
    '3:14': "The serpent is sentenced without being questioned. God interrogates those he means to deal with.",
    '3:15': "A promise buried inside a curse: the woman's seed will take the wound and crush the head.",
    '3:16': "The sentence falls on the two things she was given, childbearing and her husband, and spoils both from within.",
    '3:17': "\"Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife\" — his failure was listening to the wrong voice, not to her.",
    '3:18': "Thorns are not a new creation but the old ground turned difficult. The world still gives, grudgingly.",
    '3:19': "Bread by sweat until the dust takes him back. The sentence ends exactly where chapter two began.",
    '3:20': "Under a death sentence Adam names his wife for life. That is faith taking God at the word of verse fifteen.",
    '3:21': "God replaces the fig leaves with skins. The covering that works is one he provides, and something dies for it.",
    '3:22': "Barring the tree of life keeps them from immortality in a ruined state. The expulsion is mercy as well as judgement.",
    '3:23': "He is sent out to till the ground he came from. The work continues; only the garden is gone.",
    '3:24': "The sword keeps the way to the tree of life. The way is guarded, not destroyed, which leaves it something to be reopened.",
}

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 4 ----
    '4:1': "\"I have gotten a man from the LORD.\" Eve names the birth as a gift, and may be expecting the promised seed already.",
    '4:2': "A shepherd and a farmer. Both trades are honest, and the trouble that follows is not about the work.",
    '4:3': "Cain brings of the fruit of the ground. Nothing is said about firsts or best, and the silence is the point.",
    '4:4': "The LORD had respect to Abel and then to his offering. The man is accepted first and the gift with him.",
    '4:5': "Cain is angry at God and his face falls. Rejection at the altar is the oldest grievance in the world.",
    '4:6': "God comes to the angry man with questions before he comes with judgement.",
    '4:7': "Sin is pictured crouching at the door like a beast. Cain is warned while there is still time to shut it.",
    '4:8': "The first death in Scripture is a murder, in a field, by a brother. Worship went wrong before blood was shed.",
    '4:9': "\"Am I my brother's keeper?\" It is asked as a refusal, and the whole law will answer yes.",
    '4:10': "Blood cries from the ground. A murder nobody witnessed is heard, and the hearer is God.",
    '4:12': "The sentence is restlessness. Cain keeps his life and loses any place to put it.",
    '4:13': "\"My punishment is greater than I can bear.\" He protests the sentence and never mentions his brother.",
    '4:14': "\"From thy face shall I be hid.\" He names the real loss precisely and still does not ask for it back.",
    '4:15': "God marks a murderer to protect him. The first act of protection in the Bible is toward the guilty.",
    '4:16': "Nod means wandering, and it lies east of Eden. Cain moves further out along the road his parents took.",
    '4:17': "The man sentenced to wander builds a city and puts his son's name on it. Exile looks for something permanent.",
    '4:19': "Lamech takes two wives. What was one flesh in chapter two is now an arrangement a strong man can double.",
    '4:20': "Civilisation begins in the line of the murderer. God's gifts are not withheld from the branch that walked out.",
    '4:22': "Bronze and iron come from one forge. Every advance arrives with its weapon attached, as the next verse shows.",
    '4:23': "Lamech turns a killing into a song and performs it for his wives. Violence has learned to admire itself.",
    '4:24': "Seventy and sevenfold. God's restraint on vengeance is quoted back as a licence to multiply it.",
    '4:25': "\"Another seed instead of Abel.\" The line of promise is not rebuilt from Cain but given afresh.",
    '4:26': "Public worship begins here, in the weakest branch of the family and after the worst chapter so far.",

    # ---- Chapter 5 ----
    '5:1': "The genealogy opens by repeating that man was made in God's likeness, before listing what became of him.",
    '5:3': "Adam begets a son in his own likeness. The image is still passed on, and now it is passed on through a fallen man.",
    '5:5': "\"And he died.\" The refrain starts here and will close nearly every entry in the chapter.",
    '5:22': "Enoch walks with God for three hundred years, and it is recorded alongside ordinary fatherhood, not instead of it.",
    '5:24': "\"He was not; for God took him.\" The one man whose entry has no death in it breaks the chapter's whole rhythm.",
    '5:27': "The longest life on record still ends in the same three words as all the rest.",
    '5:29': "Lamech names his son for comfort from the cursed ground. The hope of relief is older than the flood.",
    '5:32': "Ten generations of names arrive at one man with three sons, and the story narrows to them.",

    # ---- Chapter 6 ----
    '6:2': "They saw, and they took what they chose. The verbs are Eve's at the tree, now working at scale.",
    '6:3': "God sets a term on his striving with man. Patience is real, and it is not endless.",
    '6:4': "Men of renown get one verse. Scripture records the famous and declines to be impressed by them.",
    '6:5': "Every imagination of the thoughts of the heart, only evil, continually. The diagnosis leaves no exemption.",
    '6:6': "\"It grieved him at his heart.\" God is not detached from the world he is about to judge.",
    '6:7': "The sentence reaches the beasts as well. What man was given to rule he has dragged down with him.",
    '6:8': "\"But Noah found grace in the eyes of the LORD.\" The grace is found before the character is described.",
    '6:9': "Just, perfect in his generations, walking with God — all of it said after verse eight, and in that order.",
    '6:11': "Corrupt and filled with violence. The charge is not irreligion but what men were doing to each other.",
    '6:13': "Noah is told the end of all flesh before he is told there is an ark. The bad news comes first.",
    '6:14': "Salvation arrives as a work order. Noah is given something enormous to build and told what to build it of.",
    '6:15': "Dimensions, and no rudder, sail or oar anywhere in them. The ark does not have to steer, only to float.",
    '6:16': "The door is set in the side of the ark, and there is exactly one of it.",
    '6:17': "\"I, even I, do bring a flood.\" God will not let the disaster be blamed on anything but himself.",
    '6:18': "The first mention of covenant in the Bible sits in the middle of a sentence about drowning the world.",
    '6:19': "The rescue takes in the animals. God saves more of his creation than the part that sinned against him.",
    '6:21': "Noah must gather the food. God's provision usually comes through somebody's labour and planning.",
    '6:22': "\"Thus did Noah.\" No argument, no question, no recorded reaction — the whole response fits in a line.",

    # ---- Chapter 7 ----
    '7:1': "\"Come thou.\" God does not send Noah into the ark from a distance; he calls him in to where he is.",
    '7:2': "Clean and unclean are distinguished long before Sinai. Noah is expected to know the difference already.",
    '7:3': "\"To keep seed alive.\" The purpose stated is preservation, even in the chapter that drowns the world.",
    '7:4': "Seven days' notice, then forty of rain. Judgement is announced before it falls, every time.",
    '7:5': "\"According unto all that the LORD commanded him.\" The obedience is total and it is reported without praise.",
    '7:10': "Seven days between the warning and the water. The last week of the old world looked like every other week.",
    '7:11': "The deep breaks up and heaven's windows open. The separations of the first chapter are being undone.",
    '7:13': "Eight names. At the end of this verse that is the entire human race.",
    '7:16': "\"The LORD shut him in.\" The door is closed from the outside, and Noah could not have opened it either.",
    '7:17': "The same water that destroys everything else is what lifts the ark. Judgement and rescue are one flood.",
    '7:19': "All the high hills covered. The chapter is careful to leave nowhere else anyone could have gone.",
    '7:21': "The list runs through every kind God made in chapter one, in reverse, and ends at man.",
    '7:22': "\"All in whose nostrils was the breath of life.\" The breath God gave in the garden is taken back.",
    '7:23': "\"Noah only remained alive, and they that were with him.\" Being with him was the whole of their qualification.",
    '7:24': "A hundred and fifty days of water after the rain has stopped. Deliverance takes far longer than ruin.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 8 ----
    '8:1': "\"God remembered Noah.\" Remembering in Scripture is not recalling but acting, and a wind moves over the water again.",
    '8:2': "The deep is stopped and the rain restrained. The judgement had a hand on it the whole time.",
    '8:4': "The ark rests. It never sailed anywhere; it was carried, and then it was set down.",
    '8:6': "Noah opens a window rather than the door. He looks out long before he presumes to go out.",
    '8:7': "The raven goes to and fro and never comes back. A bird that feeds on carrion finds this world habitable.",
    '8:8': "The dove is sent to look for what the raven was content to do without.",
    '8:9': "No rest for the sole of her foot, so she comes back and a hand reaches out and pulls her in.",
    '8:10': "He waits another seven days. Most of Noah's faith in this chapter looks like sitting still.",
    '8:11': "One olive leaf is enough. Hope arrives small and specific rather than in a general improvement.",
    '8:12': "This time the dove does not return, and the silence is the good news.",
    '8:13': "The ground is dry and Noah still does not step out. He came in at a word and will leave at one.",
    '8:16': "\"Go forth.\" The word that shut him in is the word that lets him out, and he waited for it.",
    '8:17': "Be fruitful and multiply, spoken over the animals. The first chapter's commission is reissued, not cancelled.",
    '8:20': "The first thing built in the new world is an altar, out of the spare clean beasts he had been told to bring.",
    '8:21': "\"The imagination of man's heart is evil from his youth.\" The reason for the flood is now the reason for mercy.",
    '8:22': "Seedtime and harvest are promised, not assumed. The ordinary year is something God has undertaken to keep.",

    # ---- Chapter 9 ----
    '9:1': "The blessing of the first chapter is spoken again over eight people, and the word \"subdue\" is quietly left out.",
    '9:2': "Fear and dread now stand between man and the animals. Dominion has become something closer to a standoff.",
    '9:3': "Meat is permitted where only herbs were given before. The new world is granted more and trusted less.",
    '9:4': "The blood is withheld. Even in what God gives away he keeps a mark that the life was his.",
    '9:5': "God says he will require the life of man, from beast and brother alike. The accounting is his, not ours.",
    '9:6': "The ground of the death penalty is the image of God in the victim, not the dignity of the one who executes it.",
    '9:7': "The answer to a world that had filled with violence is the command to fill it with people.",
    '9:9': "God establishes the covenant. Noah is told about it rather than consulted, and nothing is asked of him.",
    '9:10': "Every living creature is named a party to the covenant. The animals are not merely the setting for the story.",
    '9:11': "The promise is exact: never again by water. God does not promise that nothing will ever be judged.",
    '9:12': "A covenant is given a token. God attaches his word to something the eye can find.",
    '9:13': "A bow set in the cloud is a weapon hung up, and it is pointed away from the earth.",
    '9:15': "\"I will remember my covenant.\" The sign in the sky is aimed at God's faithfulness, not at our memory.",
    '9:18': "\"And Ham is the father of Canaan\" is dropped in early. The narrator is preparing the reader for verse 25.",
    '9:19': "Every nation the rest of this book deals with comes from three men in one family.",
    '9:20': "The man who survived the flood goes back to the cursed ground and plants a vineyard. Ordinary life resumes.",
    '9:21': "Drunk and uncovered in his tent. The new beginning fails in its first generation, in a garden, over fruit.",
    '9:22': "Ham looks, and then goes and tells. The offence is not the seeing alone but what he did with it.",
    '9:23': "They walk in backwards with a garment between them. Love covers a father's shame without inspecting it.",
    '9:24': "Noah wakes and knows. Sin done to a sleeping man does not stay a private matter.",
    '9:25': "The curse falls on Canaan, who did nothing. A father's sin lands where he least wanted it to.",
    '9:26': "The blessing is pronounced not on Shem but on Shem's God. The line of promise is marked by whom it worships.",
    '9:27': "Japheth dwelling in the tents of Shem: the outsider lodged inside another man's blessing.",
    '9:29': "Nine hundred and fifty years, and the chapter still ends with the two words from chapter five.",

    # ---- Chapter 10 ----
    '10:1': "Before any chosen nation appears, Scripture takes the trouble to list all of them.",
    '10:5': "Separate tongues and lands are already here, one chapter before Babel explains how that happened.",
    '10:8': "Nimrod is the first man in the Bible called mighty, and the word is not a compliment for long.",
    '10:9': "\"A mighty hunter before the LORD\" became a proverb. Reputation outlives the man and rarely improves him.",
    '10:10': "The beginning of his kingdom was Babel. Empire enters the record as one man's personal project.",
    '10:19': "The Canaanite border is traced as far as Sodom and Gomorrah, cities that get named here before they burn.",
    '10:21': "Eber's name is buried in a list, and the word Hebrew will come out of it.",
    '10:25': "Peleg, \"for in his days was the earth divided.\" A whole catastrophe compressed into a man's name.",
    '10:32': "Seventy nations, all from one father, all accounted for. Israel's Bible begins by claiming everybody.",

    # ---- Chapter 11 ----
    '11:1': "One language and one speech. The problem in this chapter is not that men could not cooperate.",
    '11:2': "They find a plain and they dwell there. The command was to fill the earth, and they stop at the first good ground.",
    '11:3': "Brick for stone, slime for mortar. A city made entirely of what men manufacture for themselves.",
    '11:4': "\"Let us make us a name, lest we be scattered.\" The motive is stated aloud: fame, and fear of dispersal.",
    '11:5': "The tower whose top would reach heaven is so far below it that the LORD has to come down to see.",
    '11:6': "\"Nothing will be restrained from them.\" Unlimited human capacity is named here as a danger, not an achievement.",
    '11:7': "\"Let us go down\" answers \"let us make brick.\" Two councils, and only one of them has the last word.",
    '11:8': "God scatters them, which was the one thing they built the city to prevent.",
    '11:9': "They wanted a name and they got one: Babel, confusion. The name stuck and the tower did not.",
    '11:10': "The list starts again from Shem, and the story is narrowing from all the nations back down to one line.",
    '11:19': "The lifespans halve and halve again down the column. The world after the flood is visibly running down.",
    '11:26': "Terah's three sons are named without comment. Nothing marks Abram out yet except that he is listed.",
    '11:27': "Lot is introduced here, attached to the family by an early death, and he will be trouble later.",
    '11:28': "Haran dies before his father, in Ur. The family that will be promised a land begins with a grave in the old one.",
    '11:29': "Two brothers take wives and the narrative pauses on the names. One of them is about to matter enormously.",
    '11:30': "\"But Sarai was barren; she had no child.\" Everything God promises from the next chapter on runs into this sentence.",
    '11:31': "Terah sets out for Canaan and settles in Haran. The journey Abram is called to was begun and abandoned by his father.",
    '11:32': "Terah dies in Haran, short of the land. Getting halfway is its own kind of ending.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 12 ----
    '12:1': "Country, kindred, father's house — everything is named except the destination, which stays \"a land that I will shew thee.\"",
    '12:2': "A great name is given to the man who was not building one. Babel wanted exactly this and had to be stopped.",
    '12:3': "One family is chosen so that all families are blessed. Election here is never a private arrangement.",
    '12:4': "\"So Abram departed.\" Seventy-five years old, no map, and the obedience gets one clause.",
    '12:5': "A whole household travels on one man's word from God. Faith is rarely an individual inconvenience.",
    '12:6': "\"And the Canaanite was then in the land.\" The promised country is already fully occupied by somebody else.",
    '12:7': "\"Unto thy seed will I give this land\" — to the seed, not to him. Abram builds an altar on ground he will never own.",
    '12:8': "Tents and altars. He puts down nothing permanent except places to worship.",
    '12:10': "Famine in the land of promise, immediately. The promise does not come with a guarantee of good harvests.",
    '12:11': "Abram does the arithmetic on his wife's beauty and his own safety before he crosses the border.",
    '12:13': "\"That it may be well with me for thy sake.\" He puts her in the danger and calls it a favour to him.",
    '12:15': "Sarai is taken into Pharaoh's house. The promise of a seed is now inside the harem of a foreign king.",
    '12:16': "Abram grows rich on the strength of the lie. Prosperity is no evidence that a thing was rightly done.",
    '12:17': "God plagues Pharaoh to rescue the promise from the man he made it to.",
    '12:18': "The pagan king reprimands the man of faith, and every word of the reprimand is deserved.",
    '12:20': "Abram is escorted out of Egypt with his wife and his goods, exposed and unpunished.",

    # ---- Chapter 13 ----
    '13:2': "He comes out of Egypt richer than he went in. The episode is not tidied up, and neither is the profit.",
    '13:4': "He goes back to the altar he built before, and calls on the name of the LORD there. Repentance retraces its steps.",
    '13:6': "The land could not bear them because their substance was great. Prosperity is what pulls this family apart.",
    '13:7': "The quarrel is between herdmen, and the Canaanite and the Perizzite are watching it happen.",
    '13:8': "\"For we be brethren.\" Abram ends the strife by surrendering the right he clearly holds.",
    '13:9': "The elder hands the younger first choice. A man who trusts the promise can afford to go second.",
    '13:10': "Lot lifts up his eyes and sees water, and the narrator adds what Lot could not see coming.",
    '13:12': "\"Pitched his tent toward Sodom.\" A direction of travel, and by chapter fourteen it has become an address.",
    '13:13': "The character of Sodom is stated flatly right after the choice, not before it.",
    '13:14': "The LORD speaks to Abram only after Lot is gone. Some words come after the loss and not before.",
    '13:15': "All the land, north to south, east to west, for ever. The portion Lot took was never subtracted from it.",
    '13:16': "Seed like the dust of the earth, said to a man with no children at all.",
    '13:17': "He is told to walk the land he does not own. Possession is taken by faith long before it is taken by law.",
    '13:18': "He settles at Mamre and builds another altar. The man with no title deed keeps consecrating the ground.",

    # ---- Chapter 14 ----
    '14:4': "Twelve years of paying tribute, then rebellion. The first war in the Bible is about money and who rules.",
    '14:9': "\"Four kings with five.\" A campaign that shook the region is reduced to a score, on the way to Abram.",
    '14:10': "The kings of Sodom flee into the tar pits of their own valley. The ground they fought for swallows them.",
    '14:12': "\"Lot, Abram's brother's son, who dwelt in Sodom.\" The tent pitched toward the city is now inside it.",
    '14:13': "Abram is called the Hebrew here for the first time, and he has Amorite neighbours who are his allies.",
    '14:14': "Three hundred and eighteen men born in his own house. He fights with a household, not an army.",
    '14:15': "He divides his force and strikes by night. Faith here looks a great deal like competence.",
    '14:16': "He risks everything to recover the nephew who took the better land and never asked how he was doing.",
    '14:17': "The king of Sodom comes out to meet the man who has just saved him, and he comes with an offer.",
    '14:18': "Bread and wine from a priest-king nobody has introduced. Melchizedek arrives without genealogy and leaves without one.",
    '14:19': "\"Possessor of heaven and earth.\" The blessing rests on who owns the land, not on who won the battle.",
    '14:20': "Abram tithes to a priest he has no obligation to, before any law requires a tenth of anyone.",
    '14:21': "Sodom's king wants the people and offers the goods. He values what Abram will not sell and sells what he should not.",
    '14:22': "\"I have lift up mine hand unto the LORD.\" The decision was made before the offer was on the table.",
    '14:23': "Not a thread or a shoelatchet, \"lest thou shouldest say, I have made Abram rich.\" He guards whose gift it is.",
    '14:24': "He will not impose his own vow on Aner, Eshcol and Mamre. A private conviction is kept private.",

    # ---- Chapter 15 ----
    '15:1': "\"I am thy shield, and thy exceeding great reward.\" The reward turns out to be the giver himself.",
    '15:2': "\"What wilt thou give me, seeing I go childless?\" Abram answers a promise with a complaint, and is not rebuked.",
    '15:4': "God does not adjust the promise to fit Abram's workaround. The heir will come out of his own body.",
    '15:5': "He is taken outside and told to count the stars. The promise is made in a form he cannot get to the end of.",
    '15:6': "He believed, and it was counted to him for righteousness. Righteousness is reckoned to faith, not earned by works.",
    '15:7': "God identifies himself by what he has already done. Assurance for the future is argued from the past.",
    '15:8': "\"Whereby shall I know?\" The man just credited with righteousness asks for proof in the next breath.",
    '15:9': "The answer to the question is not an argument but a covenant ceremony with animals and a knife.",
    '15:10': "The pieces are laid opposite each other to make a path. Whoever walks it is saying, let this be done to me.",
    '15:11': "Birds of prey come down on the sacrifice and Abram drives them away. Waiting on God is not idleness.",
    '15:12': "A deep sleep, and a horror of great darkness. The covenant comes with dread before it comes with comfort.",
    '15:13': "Four hundred years of slavery are foretold to the man being blessed. God tells him the cost in advance.",
    '15:14': "\"I will judge\" that nation, and they shall come out with great substance. The exodus is promised here.",
    '15:15': "Abram will die before any of this happens, and is told so plainly as part of the good news.",
    '15:16': "\"The iniquity of the Amorites is not yet full.\" The delay is patience toward the people who will lose the land.",
    '15:17': "The furnace and the lamp pass between the pieces alone. God walks the death path and Abram is asleep.",
    '15:18': "The covenant is cut and the boundaries named. Only one party moved, so only one party can break it.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 16 ----
    '16:1': "A childless wife and an Egyptian handmaid. The souvenir of Abram's worst decision is standing in the tent.",
    '16:2': "\"Abram hearkened to the voice of Sarai.\" The narrator uses the exact phrase God used of Adam in the garden.",
    '16:4': "The plan works at once and curdles at once. Hagar conceives and her mistress is despised in her eyes.",
    '16:5': "\"My wrong be upon thee.\" Sarai hands Abram the blame for the arrangement she proposed.",
    '16:6': "\"Do to her as it pleaseth thee.\" Abram will not choose between the two women and so abandons one of them.",
    '16:7': "The angel of the LORD appears for the first time in Scripture, and it is to a pregnant runaway slave.",
    '16:8': "\"Hagar, Sarai's maid.\" He knows her name and her station, and asks where she has come from and where she is going.",
    '16:9': "\"Return to thy mistress, and submit thyself.\" The hard word comes before the promise, not instead of it.",
    '16:10': "The language of Abram's promise is spoken over the slave woman's child as well.",
    '16:11': "Ishmael means God hears. Nobody in the household listened to her, and the name records who did.",
    '16:12': "The promise about her son is not flattering. God tells her the truth about the child he is giving her.",
    '16:13': "\"Thou God seest me.\" A foreign slave is the only person in Scripture who gives God a name.",
    '16:15': "Abram calls the boy Ishmael, which is the name the angel gave in the wilderness. She had told him everything.",

    # ---- Chapter 17 ----
    '17:1': "Thirteen years of silence end with \"I am the Almighty God.\" The command to walk before him follows the name.",
    '17:3': "Abraham falls on his face. The right posture arrives before he has heard the terms.",
    '17:4': "\"As for me.\" God states his own part of the covenant first, and at length, before any duty is mentioned.",
    '17:5': "The name is changed to Abraham, father of many nations, while he is the father of exactly one boy.",
    '17:6': "Kings are promised out of a man who has never owned a field he did not buy for a grave.",
    '17:7': "The content of the covenant is stated plainly: \"to be a God unto thee.\" The gift is God, and the rest follows.",
    '17:8': "An everlasting possession of \"the land wherein thou art a stranger.\" Both halves of that are true at once.",
    '17:9': "\"Thou shalt keep my covenant.\" The obligation is real and it arrives after six verses of pure promise.",
    '17:10': "The sign God chooses is hidden, permanent, and impossible to display to anyone as a credential.",
    '17:11': "A token of the covenant, cut into the flesh. The promise is attached to something that cannot be argued away.",
    '17:12': "The sign is given at eight days old. The infant consents to nothing and is marked as belonging anyway.",
    '17:14': "To refuse the sign is to break the covenant. God takes his own tokens seriously.",
    '17:15': "Sarah is renamed too. She is not a bystander to the covenant her husband is making.",
    '17:16': "\"I will bless her.\" The son must come through the barren wife, or the promise is not the promise.",
    '17:17': "Abraham falls on his face and laughs. Worship and disbelief are in the same body at the same moment.",
    '17:18': "\"O that Ishmael might live before thee.\" He asks God to bless the arrangement he already made instead.",
    '17:19': "God names the boy Laughter. The joke Abraham made in his heart becomes the child's name for ever.",
    '17:20': "Ishmael is blessed, multiplied, made a great nation. Being outside the covenant line is not being abandoned.",
    '17:21': "\"But my covenant will I establish with Isaac.\" The choice is God's, and he states it without explaining it.",
    '17:23': "\"In the selfsame day.\" A ninety-nine-year-old man obeys a painful command before the sun goes down.",
    '17:27': "Servants born in the house and slaves bought with money all carry the sign. The covenant takes in the household.",

    # ---- Chapter 18 ----
    '18:1': "The LORD appears while a man is sitting in a tent door in the heat of the day, doing nothing in particular.",
    '18:2': "He runs. An old man in the midday heat runs at the sight of three strangers he does not yet recognise.",
    '18:3': "He begs them to stay as though the favour would be theirs. Hospitality is asked for, not merely offered.",
    '18:5': "He promises a morsel of bread and then serves a calf. Generosity that understates itself is the real kind.",
    '18:8': "He stands while they eat. The host waits on the guests instead of taking his place at the table.",
    '18:9': "\"Where is Sarah thy wife?\" They know her name, and the question is asked so she will overhear the answer.",
    '18:10': "The promise is finally given a date, and Sarah hears it from behind the tent door.",
    '18:11': "The narrator states the medical facts. The point of the next verses depends on this being genuinely impossible.",
    '18:12': "Sarah laughs at the thought of pleasure, not only at the thought of a child. The loss she names is her own.",
    '18:13': "She laughed within herself, and it is quoted back to her. God hears what is said inside a person.",
    '18:14': "\"Is any thing too hard for the LORD?\" The whole book has been asking this, and here it is asked out loud.",
    '18:15': "\"I laughed not; for she was afraid.\" The denial is met with a flat contradiction and no punishment at all.",
    '18:17': "\"Shall I hide from Abraham that thing which I do?\" God deliberates about taking a man into his confidence.",
    '18:18': "The reason given for telling him is the blessing of all nations. Being let in serves somebody other than Abraham.",
    '18:19': "Election here has a job attached: he will command his children after him to do justice and judgement.",
    '18:20': "\"The cry of Sodom.\" Its sin is heard as the noise of people being wronged, not merely as an offence to God.",
    '18:21': "\"I will go down now, and see.\" The Judge of all the earth investigates before he sentences.",
    '18:22': "The men go on toward Sodom \"but Abraham stood yet before the LORD.\" He stays where the conversation is.",
    '18:23': "\"Wilt thou also destroy the righteous with the wicked?\" He argues for the city on the ground of God's justice.",
    '18:24': "He does not ask that the guilty be excused but that the many be spared for the sake of a few.",
    '18:25': "\"Shall not the Judge of all the earth do right?\" Abraham appeals to God against what he fears God might do.",
    '18:26': "God agrees to spare the whole city for fifty. The righteous are counted as a reason to hold back judgement.",
    '18:27': "\"Which am but dust and ashes.\" The boldest prayer in Genesis is also the most self-effacing.",
    '18:32': "He stops at ten. God never refuses; the bargaining ends because the man asking runs out of nerve.",
    '18:33': "The LORD goes his way as soon as the communing ends. The conversation closes at heaven's initiative.",

    # ---- Chapter 19 ----
    '19:1': "Lot is sitting in the gate, which is where the city's business was done. The sojourner has become a somebody.",
    '19:2': "They intend to stay in the street, and Lot will not hear of it. He knows exactly what the street holds.",
    '19:4': "\"Both old and young, all the people from every quarter.\" The narrator forecloses the idea of a decent remnant.",
    '19:5': "The demand is made openly at the door, with no shame and no expectation of refusal.",
    '19:6': "He goes out and shuts the door behind him. Whatever else Lot is, he puts his body between them and his guests.",
    '19:8': "Lot offers his daughters. Scripture records it and offers no defence, and the family pays for it in this chapter.",
    '19:9': "\"This one fellow came in to sojourn, and he will needs be a judge.\" Years in Sodom bought him no standing.",
    '19:10': "The rescuers pull the rescuer inside. From here on Lot does nothing for himself.",
    '19:11': "Struck blind, they still weary themselves feeling for the door. Appetite outlasts the power to satisfy it.",
    '19:13': "\"The LORD hath sent us to destroy it.\" The judgement is announced as an errand, not an outburst.",
    '19:14': "\"He seemed as one that mocked.\" Lot's warning fails because his life in the city had already contradicted it.",
    '19:15': "The angels have to hurry him. Mercy is under time pressure and the man being saved is not.",
    '19:16': "\"While he lingered\" they took him by the hand. He is dragged out of the fire he half wanted to stay in.",
    '19:17': "\"Look not behind thee.\" The command is not about curiosity but about what the heart is still attached to.",
    '19:18': "\"Oh, not so, my LORD.\" He negotiates with the angels who have hold of his hand.",
    '19:19': "He names the mercy shown him accurately and then explains why he cannot accept the terms of it.",
    '19:20': "\"Is it not a little one?\" He argues for a smaller city as though size were the problem with Sodom.",
    '19:21': "God grants him the concession. The rescue bends to accommodate a man who is still afraid of the mountain.",
    '19:22': "\"I cannot do anything till thou be come thither.\" The fire waits until the one being saved is out of it.",
    '19:24': "Brimstone and fire out of heaven, in daylight, on a plain that looked like the garden of the LORD.",
    '19:26': "She looked back and stopped there. The wife is destroyed not by the fire but by what she could not leave.",
    '19:27': "Abraham returns to the place where he had stood before the LORD. He goes back to where he prayed.",
    '19:28': "He sees the smoke and is told nothing about Lot. The intercessor is not shown the result of his prayer.",
    '19:29': "\"God remembered Abraham, and sent Lot out.\" Lot's rescue is credited to somebody else's intercession.",
    '19:30': "He ends up in the mountain cave he begged not to be sent to. The concession he won bought him nothing.",
    '19:31': "The daughters reason from Sodom's assumptions. He got them out of the city and not the city out of them.",
    '19:33': "Wine, a father uncovered, children who take advantage. Genesis has told this story once before, after the flood.",
    '19:37': "Moab is born here, and centuries later a Moabite woman named Ruth will walk into the line of David.",
    '19:38': "Ammon and Moab both trace to this cave. Israel's most persistent enemies are its own relatives.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 20 ----
    '20:2': "\"She is my sister.\" Twenty-five years, a covenant and a name change later, the same lie comes out again.",
    '20:3': "God speaks in a dream to a pagan king before he says anything to his prophet about the matter.",
    '20:4': "\"Wilt thou slay also a righteous nation?\" Abimelech pleads exactly as Abraham pleaded over Sodom.",
    '20:6': "\"I also withheld thee from sinning against me.\" God restrains evils we never find out we were spared.",
    '20:7': "The liar is still called a prophet, and the man he wronged is told to ask him to pray.",
    '20:8': "The whole court is sore afraid. The fear of God turns up in the place Abraham was certain had none.",
    '20:9': "\"Thou hast done deeds unto me that ought not to be done.\" A second pagan king schools the man of faith.",
    '20:11': "\"Surely the fear of God is not in this place.\" He assumed the worst about strangers and acted on the assumption.",
    '20:12': "Abraham's defence is that the lie was technically true. Half a truth is the most durable kind of falsehood.",
    '20:13': "It was not a moment's panic. He had arranged this with Sarah for every place they would ever come to.",
    '20:15': "\"My land is before thee: dwell where it pleaseth thee.\" A foreign king offers what God had already promised.",
    '20:16': "The silver and the public statement are meant to clear Sarah's name. The narrator adds, \"thus she was reproved.\"",
    '20:17': "Abraham prays for the household he endangered, and God heals it. The office survives the man's failure in it.",
    '20:18': "Wombs closed because of Sarah, in the chapter immediately before hers is opened.",

    # ---- Chapter 21 ----
    '21:1': "\"As he had said\" and \"as he had spoken.\" The verse says twice that nothing happened except what was promised.",
    '21:2': "At the set time. Twenty-five years of waiting end on a date God had already named.",
    '21:4': "The first thing done for the child of promise is to mark him with the sign of the covenant.",
    '21:6': "\"God hath made me to laugh.\" The laugh behind the tent door has been turned into something she can share.",
    '21:7': "\"Who would have said unto Abraham?\" Nobody would have, which is the whole point of the birth.",
    '21:8': "The feast is at the weaning, not the birth. Joy in this family has learned to wait for the child to be safe.",
    '21:9': "Sarah sees Ishmael mocking, and what happens next begins with a look rather than a word.",
    '21:10': "\"Cast out this bondwoman and her son.\" Paul will take this up as law and promise refusing to share a house.",
    '21:11': "\"The thing was very grievous in Abraham's sight.\" He loved the son he got by unbelief, and losing him hurt.",
    '21:12': "\"In Isaac shall thy seed be called.\" The line runs through the promise, never through the arrangement.",
    '21:13': "Ishmael is made a nation \"because he is thy seed.\" God honours the tie Abraham is being asked to cut.",
    '21:14': "Bread and a bottle of water. Everything a slave woman and her son are sent into the desert with fits on one shoulder.",
    '21:15': "The water runs out and she puts the boy under a shrub. Nothing in the desert is impressed by a promise.",
    '21:16': "\"Let me not see the death of the child.\" She sits a bowshot off and weeps, which is all she has left to do.",
    '21:17': "God hears the lad, not the mother, \"where he is.\" Grace meets a person at the exact place of his need.",
    '21:18': "\"Arise, lift up the lad, and hold him.\" The rescue asks her to pick up the child she had given up on.",
    '21:19': "God opened her eyes and she saw a well. The water was there the whole time; the seeing was the gift.",
    '21:20': "\"God was with the lad.\" The boy outside the covenant line is not outside God's care.",
    '21:21': "His mother finds him a wife out of Egypt. Hagar's son is raised back toward the country she came from.",
    '21:22': "\"God is with thee in all that thou doest.\" A foreign king reads Abraham's life more accurately than Abraham does.",
    '21:23': "Abimelech wants it under oath. He has dealt with this man before and would rather not rely on his word alone.",
    '21:25': "Abraham reproves the king he is making peace with. The covenant is not a reason to leave the theft unmentioned.",
    '21:30': "Seven lambs handed over as a witness that he dug the well. He pays for evidence of what was already his.",
    '21:31': "Beersheba, the well of the oath. The name commemorates a quarrel settled rather than a victory won.",
    '21:33': "He plants a tree in a land he does not own and calls on the everlasting God. Both halves of that are deliberate.",
    '21:34': "\"Abraham sojourned in the Philistines' land many days.\" He is still a guest in the country deeded to him.",

    # ---- Chapter 22 ----
    '22:1': "\"God did tempt Abraham.\" The word is test, and the answer he gives before he knows the question is \"here I am.\"",
    '22:2': "\"Thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest.\" The command names everything it is about to cost him.",
    '22:3': "He rose up early and split the wood himself. The obedience comes with no delay and no one else's hands on it.",
    '22:4': "Three days on the road. Whatever this obedience was, it was not a decision made in a single moment of feeling.",
    '22:5': "\"We will go yonder and worship, and come again to you.\" He says come again, and he says it about both of them.",
    '22:6': "The son carries the wood for his own burnt offering, and \"they went both of them together.\"",
    '22:7': "\"Where is the lamb?\" It is the only thing Isaac says in the chapter, and it is the question the Bible answers.",
    '22:8': "\"God will provide himself a lamb.\" Whether Abraham knew how true that was, the sentence outran him.",
    '22:9': "He builds the altar, lays the wood in order, and binds his son. Every step is done carefully, which is the horror.",
    '22:10': "The knife is in his hand. Scripture does not spare the reader the moment, and does not explain it either.",
    '22:11': "\"Abraham, Abraham.\" The name twice, because one would not have been enough to stop the hand.",
    '22:12': "\"Now I know that thou fearest God.\" The test proved something to be seen, not something God had to learn.",
    '22:13': "A ram caught in a thicket, offered \"in the stead of his son.\" Substitution is named outright on this mountain.",
    '22:14': "Jehovahjireh: in the mount of the LORD it shall be seen. The place is named for the provision, not the ordeal.",
    '22:16': "\"By myself have I sworn.\" God binds himself by the only thing greater than the promise, which is God.",
    '22:17': "Stars and sand, and a seed possessing the gate of his enemies. The promise enlarges on the far side of the altar.",
    '22:18': "All nations blessed in his seed, \"because thou hast obeyed.\" The blessing of the world is tied to one man's yes.",
    '22:19': "\"So Abraham returned unto his young men.\" Isaac is not mentioned again, and the silence is loud.",
    '22:23': "Rebekah is named at the end of a family list, just after the mountain. The next generation is already arranged.",

    # ---- Chapter 23 ----
    '23:1': "Sarah is the only woman in Scripture whose age at death is recorded. The narrator counts her years deliberately.",
    '23:2': "Abraham came to mourn and to weep. Faith in the resurrection is not offered here as a reason not to grieve.",
    '23:3': "\"Abraham stood up from before his dead.\" He rises out of his grief and goes to do the necessary business.",
    '23:4': "\"I am a stranger and a sojourner with you.\" He says it in the land God swore to give him, and it is accurate.",
    '23:6': "\"Thou art a mighty prince among us.\" The Hittites rate him higher than he rates himself.",
    '23:7': "He bows to the people of the land. The man with the deed from God negotiates like any other foreigner.",
    '23:9': "He asks for the cave at full value. A gift would have left the title arguable, and he wants it beyond argument.",
    '23:11': "\"The field give I thee.\" An offer made in public that both men know is not an offer.",
    '23:15': "\"What is that betwixt me and thee?\" Four hundred shekels named as a trifle, by a man who expects to be paid.",
    '23:16': "Abraham weighs out the full asking price without haggling. He buys peace and a clear title in one payment.",
    '23:17': "The field, the cave and every tree in the borders. The deed is recorded in detail because it is the first one.",
    '23:19': "The first thing Abraham ever owns in the promised land is a grave, and he puts his wife in it.",
    '23:20': "Made sure by the sons of Heth. A burying place is the whole of the inheritance he holds when he dies.",
})
